feat: the npm launcher, under version control at last

This source was published to npm as `laya-mcp@0.1.0` and `0.1.1` without ever
being in a repository, which is a gap worth closing before it is forgotten: the
published tarball was the only copy of it.

The launcher does one thing — find a Python interpreter that can import
`laya_mcp`, hand it stdio, and stay out of the way. It deliberately does not
install anything, does not buffer or parse the MCP stream it is proxying, and
does not spawn a Python process per tool call.

0.1.1 fixed a real discovery bug. On Windows, `python` on PATH is often the
Microsoft Store execution alias, which is a stub that cannot import anything and
which sits ahead of the real interpreter. The launcher therefore concluded the
package was not installed while `pip show laya-mcp` succeeded on the same machine.
It now honours `LAYACORE_PYTHON` and `VIRTUAL_ENV` before guessing at PATH, and
on failure it names the interpreters it tried rather than only reporting that
nothing was found.

Verified: installed from the registry into a scratch directory, driven against a
virtualenv with the Python package present, and the full chain reached the GPU —
`doctor` reported `torch 2.11.0+cu128` and `RTX 5060 (sm_120)` with a real op run
on the device.
